====================================================================================================
v130  WHEN DOES THE CIRCUIT SAVING PAY?  the break-even overhead
====================================================================================================
  cost = r_circ * circuits + r_shot * shots. The circuits/shots trade is
  measured; which side wins is a property of the MACHINE. TIER A.
  Baseline is parameter-shift ON THE SAME DATA REGISTER (1+4M circuits),
  not naive per-sample PS (2M|D|) - the latter overstates by |D|/2.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1  find shot budgets that give the two arms the SAME accuracy
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      arm            shots/circ   circuits   total shots   mean cos
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      qlto_qml          1024        3           3072      +0.6979
      qlto_qml          4096        3          12288      +0.8936
      qlto_qml         16384        3          49152      +0.9541
      param-shift        256       49          12544      +0.9556
      param-shift       1024       49          50176      +0.9789
      param-shift       4096       49         200704      +0.9968

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2  the break-even per-circuit overhead
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  For a matched pair, QLTO is cheaper iff
      r_circ * (C_ps - C_q)  >  r_shot * (S_q - S_ps)
  i.e.  r_circ / r_shot  >  (S_q - S_ps) / (C_ps - C_q)   [shot-equivalents]

      target cos    qlto (C,S)        p-shift (C,S)      break-even r_circ/r_shot
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      +0.9541      (  3,   49152)   ( 49,   12544)             795.8 shots

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  BREAK-EVEN: QLTO is the cheaper route to the same accuracy only when one
  circuit costs more than about 796 shots.

  WHAT THAT MEANS ON REAL MACHINES. A superconducting shot is order 1-100
  microseconds. 796 shots is therefore roughly 0.8 ms to 0.1 s of pure
  shot time. Cloud per-job overhead - queue, compile, waveform upload,
  recalibration - is SECONDS TO MINUTES, so the inequality holds by orders
  of magnitude and QLTO wins comfortably. That is the regime every number
  in this project was measured in.

  AND IT INVERTS ON THE HARDWARE THE QUESTION IS ABOUT. A local accelerator
  - photonic, NV-diamond, anything sitting inside the machine rather than
  behind a queue - exists precisely to drive r_circ toward the cost of
  reloading a pulse sequence, i.e. toward a few shot-equivalents. Below
  796 the trade reverses and QLTO is simply paying ~4x the shots for a
  saving worth nothing.

  SO THE ADVANTAGE IS LARGEST IN THE REGIME PEOPLE WANT TO ESCAPE AND
  SMALLEST IN THE ONE THEY ARE BUILDING TOWARD. That is not a reason to
  stop - queues will exist for a long time - but it does mean the
  circuit-count framing has a shelf life, and that any claim resting on it
  should name r_circ/r_shot as the assumption it depends on.

  THIS SAYS NOTHING ABOUT QUANTUM VS CLASSICAL COST. Both are Theta(D) in
  operations on the data axis (Part III), so a cost comparison reduces to
  cost-per-operation, and that is a hardware-physics question this file
  does not touch: it compares two QUANTUM methods on one machine.

  SCOPE. N_sys=3, |D|=8, M=12, G=1, 3 seeds, 5 epochs, no noise model,
  no hardware, no vendor timings. The microsecond figures above are
  order-of-magnitude context, NOT measurements. Break-even scales with M:
  more parameters means more circuits saved, so the threshold falls and
  QLTO holds up longer as r_circ drops. At M=12 it is what it is.
